-- A visit is kept, so `never (Visit where minutes > 600)` is a constraint: Reference §21 says
-- "A prohibition over a stored (non-transient) act is also a constraint, because committing a
-- stored act is the record." `never (MakeDeposit where ...)` over the transient act is a
-- refusal, and `never (Deposit where ...)` over the stored shape is a constraint. `velle compare`
-- from an empty spec (an empty file) to this file prints, under "What was edited", these lines
-- among others. Each is followed in the report by its "In the spec:" line, left out here.
--
--   - A Visit is now refused when its minutes is above 600.
--   - A MakeDeposit is now refused when its amount is above 1000.
--   - A Deposit is now forbidden when its amount is above 5000.
--
-- `velle describe` says the same under "What is never allowed": "A Visit is refused when its
-- minutes is above 600." The constraint over the kept act gets the refusal's word, "refused",
-- and the constraint over the shape gets "forbidden", so a reader cannot tell from the words
-- that the Visit line binds the books as the Deposit line does.
act Member { name: text }

act Visit {
    member: one Member
    minutes: integer
}

never (Visit where minutes > 600)

transient act MakeDeposit {
    member: one Member
    amount: decimal
}

shape Deposit {
    member: one Member
    amount: decimal
}

rule RecordDeposit when MakeDeposit {
    Deposit from { member: member, amount: amount }
}

never (MakeDeposit where amount > 1000)

never (Deposit where amount > 5000)
